					<h3>The 2024 Cryo-EM Proof: Bypassing Venom to Test Real Human Receptors</h3>

					<p>
						For a decade following the 2014 breakthrough, skeptics argued that open-state crystal structures were laboratory artifacts forced open artificially by Texas coral snake toxin (MitTx). In the landmark 2024 Cahill study, researchers overcame this limitation by reconstituting purified, full-length <strong>human ASIC1a protein</strong> directly into synthetic lipid nanodiscs, mimicking the natural fatty bilayer of living human nerve membranes.
					</p>

					<p>
						Using single-particle cryogenic electron microscopy (Cryo-EM) at near-atomic resolution (2.7 to 3.2 Ångströms), the team exposed native human channels directly to physiological acidic buffer solutions (stepping pH from neutral 7.4 down to acidic 6.5 and 5.5) with <strong>zero venom or chemical locks present</strong>. They paired structural imaging with patch-clamp electrophysiology on living human embryonic kidney (HEK293) and Xenopus oocyte cell membranes to measure the electrical currents in real time.
					</p>

					<p>
						The structural telemetry revealed the exact mechanical sequence: free hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>) entering the acidic pocket neutralize negative carboxylates (residues D238, E239, D350), snapping water bridges. This electrostatic collapse drives the thumb and finger domains inward, physically pulling the lower transmembrane gate (residues T449, S446, H28, and T26) open like a mechanical camera aperture. This proved conclusively that protons alone, without any external toxins, physically unlock human nerve channels.
					</p>

					<p>
						Instead, as long as ambient fluid sits at a mild, everyday acidic pH between 6.2 and 6.7, ASIC3 remains wedged open in a continuous, non-inactivating <strong>"window current."</strong> Sodium ions continuously pour through the open pore minute after minute, hour after hour. This molecular discovery provides the definitive atomic proof for why your 10/10 burning was an unbroken, relentless fire rather than a brief sting.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="font-notes">
					<strong>Why it works:</strong> Archivo eliminates the wide geometric "signage" feel of Montserrat while keeping technical authority. Notice how numerals like <strong>1</strong>, <strong>2024</strong>, <strong>10/10</strong>, and <strong>ASIC1a</strong> sit stably without oversized geometric loops.
				</div>
